{"nodes": [{"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_structural_sharing", "label": "Structural Sharing", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "Mental Model", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "An update copies only the root-to-change path and shares every untouched subtree by reference, so versioning costs proportional to the change (Theta(log n)) rather than to the data (Theta(n))."}, {"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_path_copying", "label": "Path Copying", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "Mechanics", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "n.right.clone() on an Arc bumps a refcount, so an arbitrarily large subtree is copied in constant time. Cost is exactly the depth, so balance matters MORE than for a mutable tree: depth 99,999 means 99,999 allocations per insert."}, {"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_hamt", "label": "HAMT (Hash Array Mapped Trie)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "HAMT \u2014 the persistent hash map", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A trie over hash bits with 32-way branching: a 32-bit bitmap plus a packed child array, indexed via popcount(bitmap & ((1<<i)-1)). ~4 levels for 1M entries, and branching on hash bits gives uniform depth regardless of key distribution."}, {"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_rrb_tree", "label": "RRB-Tree (Persistent Vector)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "RRB-trees", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "32-way branching trie indexed by position: Theta(log32 n) index/update/push/pop plus Theta(log n) concatenation and slicing, which a flat Vec cannot do at all. The 'relaxed' part (partially full nodes) is what makes concatenation efficient."}, {"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_arc_make_mut", "label": "Arc::make_mut Copy-on-Write", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "The Rust-specific angle", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Mutates in place when the refcount is 1 and clones otherwise \u2014 persistent semantics at mutable speed for the common path. Has a Theta(n) cliff on the first write after a snapshot, so frequent snapshotting gets the worst of both."}, {"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_persistence_levels", "label": "Persistence Levels (Partial / Full / Confluent)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "The Invariant", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Confluent persistence is harder than full because merging can create sharing patterns that break the Theta(log n) bound. Git with merges is confluently persistent."}, {"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_immutable_is_sync_for_free", "label": "Immutable Structures Are Sync For Free", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "The Invariant", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Arc<Node> with no interior mutability is Send + Sync, so a persistent structure is concurrently readable with zero synchronization. That property, not the versioning, is what makes it worth its 2-10x read constant factor in Rust."}, {"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_cas_on_root", "label": "CAS on the Root When Publishing a Version", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "Pitfalls in Depth", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Immutability makes reading lock-free but 'derive a new version and store it' is a read-modify-write on the shared root pointer, which is not atomic just because the data is immutable. Two threads deriving from one root silently lose an update. Fix: compare-and-swap on the root, retrying if the base changed \u2014 the same optimistic-concurrency pattern as expected_version in event sourcing."}, {"id": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_retained_versions_leak", "label": "Accidentally Retained Versions", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "data-structures-and-algorithms/persistent-immutable-structures/learning.md", "source_location": "Pitfalls in Depth", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Because every version shares structure, a single retained old root pins all structure reachable from it. Memory grows without bound and profiling shows no leak because every allocation is genuinely reachable. Bound the version history explicitly."}], "edges": [{"source": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_path_copying", "target": "data_structures_and_algorithms_persistent_immutable_structures_learning_structural_sharing", "relation": "implements", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score"